Description: avfilter/tonemap_cuda: read and write samples as whole codes
 tonemap_cuda wrote every 10-bit sample about two codes low. A black
 Dolby Vision frame came out as Y 62, Cb 510, Cr 510 where BT.2100
 gives 64, 512, 512. In 8 bits, black tone mapped to BT.709 came out
 as Y 15, Cb 127, Cr 127. The conversions between integer codes and
 floats did it:
 .
 rgb2yuv() rounded a narrow-range sample to a 16-bit code and then
 subtracted output_quantization_offset, 64/65535 for 10-bit output,
 which is one whole 10-bit code.
 .
 sample_to_ushort() multiplied that 16-bit fraction by 1023, where
 65535/64 = 1023.98 undoes it, and truncated instead of rounding.
 .
 A 16-bit texture returns an n-bit code as code * 2^(16-n) / 65535.
 yuv2rgb() added input_quantization_offset, one whole code, where a
 factor of 65535 / ((2^n - 1) * 2^(16-n)) gives the code / (2^n - 1)
 that its range scaling takes. The Dolby Vision path used the texture
 value as it was, where the reshaping curves take code / (2^n - 1).
 .
 Full-range chroma was centred on 0.5, where BT.2100 table 9 puts the
 achromatic code at 2^(n-1) of 2^n - 1.
 .
 Scale narrow-range output at the output depth as BT.2100 table 9
 defines it and round once, to the nearest code, when the sample is
 written, after the dither and the chroma average. Clamp to the sample
 range, scale every texture read to code / (2^n - 1), and centre
 full-range chroma on 2^(n-1) / (2^n - 1). The quantization offset and
 output constants are no longer needed.
 .
 Rounding once also lets the luma dither act on 8-bit narrow-range
 output. It had no effect there, because the value it received was
 already a whole code.
